Subject: [PATCH] fonts: use the Windows script fallback table under a Windows profile

Patch 0044 lifted Windows' Simplified Han candidate list onto Linux for a
Windows profile and left every other script asking Fontconfig with the
unmodified locale. So a persona claiming Windows picked the host's family for
Japanese kana, Traditional Han, Hangul, Cyrillic, Greek, Thai and Latin
whenever the requested face did not cover a character. That is readable
without a permission prompt: measureText over a string containing one
uncovered codepoint returns the fallback face's advances, and those advances
are the host's, not the claimed machine's.

This replaces the single-script predicate with the whole upstream Windows
table, copied from win/font_fallback_win.cc InitializeScriptFontMap without
edits: 70 candidate lists over 74 script entries. Nothing here is authored.
The candidate order is Windows' order, including the Noto entries Windows
lists first and rarely has, because inventing a different order would be a
new policy rather than the platform's.

Three pieces of the surrounding policy are kept as 0044 had them. The unified
Han script is resolved through LayoutLocale, so the same codepoint reaches the
Simplified, Traditional or Japanese list according to the content, accept and
system locale, which is what Windows does before it consults the table.
Unassigned, common and inherited codepoints carry no script to look up and are
recovered through Character::GetScriptBasedOnUnicodeBlock, the same recovery
font_fallback_win.cc's GetScript() performs. And the selection guard is
unchanged: the first candidate whose real localized family name matches is
taken, a Fontconfig substitution for an absent candidate is rejected by that
name check, and if the chosen family turns out not to cover the character the
existing Fontconfig path runs instead. That ordering -- choose an available
family, then test coverage -- is Windows' ordering.

The Windows reference capture measures Latin strings only
(resources/fingerprints/raw/windows-chrome-20260910T140813Z.json,
fonts.metrics, 76 Latin family names), so there is no captured non-Latin
advance to compare a chosen family against. That is precisely why the table is
copied rather than derived: the source is the control.

What remains true afterwards: 28 of the 74 entries name at least one family
the Windows font packs in resources/profiles/dispersion/font_packs.json can
provision, which is the CJK group, the Times New Roman scripts (Latin, Greek,
Cyrillic), Thai, Arabic and the Segoe UI and Segoe UI Symbol scripts. The
other 46 name families no pack carries -- Nirmala UI, Segoe UI Historic,
Leelawadee UI, David, Sylfaen, Gadugi, Mongolian Baiti and the rest -- so on a
host without those files those scripts keep the host's Fontconfig fallback, as
they did before this patch. Provisioning the files through
fonts.provisioned_directory is what closes them; no source change can.

No font file, fontconfig configuration, profile field, schema, switch or
JavaScript accessor is touched. Without a Windows profile
WindowsFallbackFamilies returns an empty span on its first check and the
function runs exactly as upstream does.

Primary source at Chromium 79460ebecaa5625e57a5fb679a735659e73dc687:
- platform/fonts/win/font_fallback_win.cc: InitializeScriptFontMap (the
  table), GetScript (the unassigned/common/inherited recovery).
- platform/text/layout_locale.cc: LocaleForHan, GetScriptForHan.
Paths above are under third_party/blink/renderer/.

Ledger: fonts.fallback-fonts.
